  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nFog... Similar to yesterday, fog bank has been slow to lift along\nthe lakeshore through the late morning and early afternoon as \nonshore flow persists. However, most inland areas are now seeing \nmainly low stratus as mixing increases. Dense fog regime looks to \ncome to an end tonight as southerly flow increases out ahead of an\napproaching cold front, though could still see some patchy dense \nfog along the lakeshore into Saturday morning. However, would \nventure to say that widespread advisory level fog does not appear \nlikely.\n\nRain chances... Majority of the next week will be characterized by\nperiodic light rain chances as several transient shortwaves and\nweak frontal boundaries migrate through the area. First round will\nbe Saturday into Sunday as moisture return along a slow-moving \nboundary nudges PWATs into the 1 to 1.25\" range (~95th\npercentile). Upper jet energy working in tandem with front in the\nvicinity should then keep on and off rain chances in the forecast\nthrough at least the middle of next week. However, overall \nrainfall amounts should be held to a minimum with high pressure in\nthe area and lack of stronger forcing at the surface. Would\nventure to say that most areas will see less than half an inch of\nQPF throughout the entire forecast period.\n\nTemperatures... Arctic high sliding south in the wake of the front\nMonday into Tuesday will bring about a spell of below average\ntemperatures during the middle of next week, with highs only \ngetting into the mid 40s to mid 50s. However, cooler temperatures \nwill be short-lived as return flow sets up on Wednesday, warming\nhighs back up into the 50s and 60s. Monday night lows will fall\ninto the low to mid 30s across the north, though little to no impacts\nare expected since the growing season has come to an end. Looking\nahead, slightly above normal temperatures are favored toward the\nend of the month and into November as upper flow flattens out,\nthough there is still uncertainty this far out as shown in \nensemble spread.",
